\section{QuickSort}
\begin{note}
Quicksort is famous for having an $O(n^2)$ worst case runtime,
but also usually being faster than merge sort.
This is for two reasons:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The worst case runtime rarely happens.
    When the input list is randomly arranged,
    quicksort has average runtime $O(n\log n)$ which matches merge sort.
\item Quicksort has a small \emph{constant factor} which makes it fast in practice.
\end{enumerate}
Average case runtime analysis requires tracking probabilities and is beyond the scope of this class.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
There are many variations of quicksort that select their pivot differently.
The main reason for choosing different pivots is to make the code robust against different types of ``worst case'' inputs.
\end{note}
